1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,100
Alright, I finally found the secret.

2
00:00:08,100 --> 00:00:11,920
I did, and I'm going to share it with you for nothing.

3
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:16,760
But this is how to make a million dollars being a scout leader.

4
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:22,500
Okay, you got a pencil, paper, are you listening?

5
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:28,160
This is how you're going to make a million dollars as a scout leader.

6
00:00:28,660 --> 00:00:30,660
Get two million dollars.

7
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,660
Hey, this guy got some 79.

8
00:00:58,160 --> 00:01:06,120
Welcome back to the Scout National Podcast.

9
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:07,120
This is Clark Green.

10
00:01:07,120 --> 00:01:09,720
Hey, it's summertime, and I'm traveling and camping.

11
00:01:09,720 --> 00:01:13,700
I've got articles and podcasts lined up to publish automatically while I'm gone, but

12
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:19,880
I won't be online very often, and it will take quite a while to moderate any comments

13
00:01:19,880 --> 00:01:23,200
that you leave on the blog or reply to email or anything like that.

14
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:27,920
But, you know, do keep in touch, and I'll catch up when we're all back home.

15
00:01:28,800 --> 00:01:34,840
This time around, we have some favorites from podcasts over the past year.

16
00:01:34,840 --> 00:01:38,880
In Scout Mastership, in seven minutes or less, we're going to be talking about the Patrol

17
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,800
Method, which is something we talk about a lot.

18
00:01:41,800 --> 00:01:48,600
But I found this little piece from a past podcast, and I thought it was worthy of re-broadcasting

19
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:49,600
for you this summer.

20
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:55,840
Then we have a Scout Masters Minute about Ernest Thompson Seaton, and finally a story

21
00:01:55,840 --> 00:01:57,760
about a clean camp.

22
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:02,620
So I think that'll be plenty for a summer podcast.

23
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:04,120
Let's get started, shall we?

24
00:02:04,120 --> 00:02:08,759
Scout Mastership in seven minutes or less.

25
00:02:16,320 --> 00:02:20,040
This is from Baden Powell's Outlook.

26
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:22,480
This is something that's not too hard to find online.

27
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,080
You know, Google Baden Powell's Outlook.

28
00:02:26,080 --> 00:02:29,360
And this is from June 1910.

29
00:02:29,360 --> 00:02:33,280
So this is quite early in the scouting movement.

30
00:02:33,280 --> 00:02:38,320
He says this, the best progress is made in those troops where power and responsibility

31
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:41,840
are really put into the hands of the patrol leaders.

32
00:02:41,840 --> 00:02:47,800
It is the secret of success with many Scout Masters, when once they have half a dozen

33
00:02:47,800 --> 00:02:53,320
patrol leaders really doing their work as if they were assistant Scout Masters.

34
00:02:53,320 --> 00:02:57,280
The Scout Masters find themselves able to go on and increase the size of their troops

35
00:02:57,280 --> 00:03:01,920
by starting new patrols or adding recruits to existing ones.

36
00:03:01,920 --> 00:03:05,600
Expect a great deal of your patrol leaders, and nine times out of ten they will play

37
00:03:05,600 --> 00:03:07,160
up to your expectation.

38
00:03:07,160 --> 00:03:11,320
But if you are going to always nurse them and not trust them to do things well, you'll

39
00:03:11,320 --> 00:03:15,079
never get them to do anything on their own initiative.

40
00:03:15,079 --> 00:03:20,280
That's Baden Powell, way back when, a hundred years ago.

41
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:26,200
At this week's troop meeting, I counted eight active assistant Scout Masters.

42
00:03:26,200 --> 00:03:32,400
Now following Baden Powell's advice, we often have comparatively little to do.

43
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:34,640
We don't do a lot of instructing.

44
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:40,640
I mean maybe two or three times in the past year, we've had an adult from our pool

45
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:44,320
of Scout Masters instructing the Scouts.

46
00:03:44,320 --> 00:03:47,600
And we do next to no program planning.

47
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:52,760
All of these things are in the firm grip of our youth leadership.

48
00:03:52,760 --> 00:03:53,880
So what do we do?

49
00:03:53,880 --> 00:03:57,840
What do we do at troop meetings?

50
00:03:57,840 --> 00:04:02,240
Well, three assistants are working hard on recruiting.

51
00:04:02,240 --> 00:04:06,480
One is following the progress of several Eagle projects and applications.

52
00:04:06,480 --> 00:04:11,240
I was meeting with a visiting Weeblows parent, and the remainder were observing

53
00:04:11,240 --> 00:04:15,680
the proceedings of the troop meeting from a respectful distance.

54
00:04:15,680 --> 00:04:17,399
And that's it.

55
00:04:17,399 --> 00:04:18,919
That's really it.

56
00:04:18,919 --> 00:04:21,839
There's not a lot for us to do.

57
00:04:21,839 --> 00:04:25,840
Now I know Scout Masters spend an inordinate amount of time worrying over their

58
00:04:25,840 --> 00:04:30,679
troop meetings, and they sometimes work on the entertainment

59
00:04:30,679 --> 00:04:33,799
quality of the meeting.

60
00:04:33,799 --> 00:04:37,799
So far as I can tell, a lot of them are actually running the meetings,

61
00:04:37,799 --> 00:04:39,280
and this is really a big no-no.

62
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:43,679
A troop meeting is not a television show hosted by the Scout Master

63
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:48,000
or a podcast where I get to talk and talk and talk.

64
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,120
No.

65
00:04:50,120 --> 00:04:54,840
Here's some thoughts from an old Scout Masters handbook that I found on

66
00:04:54,840 --> 00:04:57,040
theinquiry.net.

67
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:59,320
And if you want to go there and check it out, it's great.

68
00:04:59,320 --> 00:05:03,360
It's got lots of old school resources on scouting.

69
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:09,640
Inquiry-I-N-Q-U-I-R-Y.net.

70
00:05:09,640 --> 00:05:12,800
That'll get you to the website.

71
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:15,960
Here's what this advice said.

72
00:05:15,960 --> 00:05:19,640
As the years have gone by, some Scout Masters have set troop

73
00:05:19,640 --> 00:05:22,560
meetings on too high a pedestal.

74
00:05:22,560 --> 00:05:25,440
They have spoken of it reverently, and they spent hours and

75
00:05:25,440 --> 00:05:28,240
hours in perfecting his programs, and have seemed to

76
00:05:28,240 --> 00:05:31,200
consider that the conducting of a troop meeting successfully once

77
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:33,960
a week was the whole idea.

78
00:05:33,960 --> 00:05:38,120
And the purpose of the Scout Movement is running a fine

79
00:05:38,120 --> 00:05:39,760
lively meeting on Friday nights or

80
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:41,720
Tuesdays or Thursdays.

81
00:05:41,720 --> 00:05:44,880
And their work was done then for the week.

82
00:05:44,880 --> 00:05:49,360
There never was a falser idol set up than this glorification of

83
00:05:49,360 --> 00:05:52,600
the weekly troop meeting to the exclusion of other forms of

84
00:05:52,600 --> 00:05:53,960
scouting.

85
00:05:53,960 --> 00:05:57,760
We must keep clearly in mind at all times that the weekly

86
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:00,160
troop meeting is but a means to an end.

87
00:06:00,160 --> 00:06:02,320
It is not an end in itself.

88
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:05,760
Its business is not to be the scouting of your troop for

89
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:06,560
the week.

90
00:06:06,560 --> 00:06:11,000
Its business is to make scouting for a week.

91
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,200
To inspire it, to pep it up, to give it purpose and

92
00:06:14,200 --> 00:06:17,680
activities, to make it extend all through the week in each

93
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:20,680
patrols and each boy's life.

94
00:06:20,680 --> 00:06:23,800
We can't accomplish much actual work in an hour and a

95
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:26,720
half or two hours every week, but we can use those

96
00:06:26,720 --> 00:06:30,680
hours to motivate every other hour of the same period.

97
00:06:30,680 --> 00:06:34,080
And by motivate, we mean to stimulate activity by

98
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:38,320
providing a motive to every boy and every patrol.

99
00:06:38,320 --> 00:06:41,159
The troop meeting can have great value by simply

100
00:06:41,159 --> 00:06:44,360
bringing the boys together for a common experience.

101
00:06:44,360 --> 00:06:48,040
It can make them feel they belong together as part of

102
00:06:48,040 --> 00:06:51,920
the whole big scouting brotherhood.

103
00:06:51,920 --> 00:06:56,440
Well, I read that and I could see myself in a couple of

104
00:06:56,440 --> 00:06:58,360
the comments there.

105
00:06:58,360 --> 00:07:02,560
Yeah, big, lively, fun troop meeting, got to be

106
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:06,440
rocking and rolling all the times, got to be something

107
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:08,880
exciting and interesting and things like that.

108
00:07:08,880 --> 00:07:13,600
Well, that's really not what we're looking for.

109
00:07:13,600 --> 00:07:17,320
It's great if that happens, but hopefully it's the

110
00:07:17,320 --> 00:07:20,040
scouts that have made that happen.

111
00:07:20,040 --> 00:07:23,360
And we talked before about a matter of perspective as to

112
00:07:23,360 --> 00:07:26,880
what was good and what looked like it was orderly and

113
00:07:26,880 --> 00:07:33,640
useful for scouts according to our lights as full

114
00:07:33,680 --> 00:07:36,680
grown adult men.

115
00:07:36,680 --> 00:07:40,599
And we've talked that scouts have a very different idea

116
00:07:40,599 --> 00:07:44,479
of that, what they consider to be fun and challenging,

117
00:07:44,479 --> 00:07:47,479
we might just pass by.

118
00:07:47,479 --> 00:07:50,919
But if we have, as Baden Powell said, put the full

119
00:07:50,919 --> 00:07:53,800
responsibility of what happens in a troop meeting on

120
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:57,120
our youth leadership, they're going to shine.

121
00:07:57,120 --> 00:07:59,760
They're going to rise to your expectations and

122
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:01,479
surpass them.

123
00:08:01,480 --> 00:08:05,400
As long as you look at it in the right perspective.

124
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:10,520
So that's the heart of what a scout master's role is

125
00:08:10,520 --> 00:08:12,600
at a troop meeting.

126
00:08:12,600 --> 00:08:18,000
You know, there's a widely circulated little narrative

127
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:20,160
called the one minute scout master, and I think it was

128
00:08:20,160 --> 00:08:23,000
based on some management science thing.

129
00:08:23,000 --> 00:08:26,120
We're going to talk about that in the next podcast.

130
00:08:26,120 --> 00:08:29,840
But basically, you know, one of the tests for a

131
00:08:29,840 --> 00:08:33,760
successful troop meeting with you as a scout master is

132
00:08:33,760 --> 00:08:37,720
you sit in a chair and you wait.

133
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:40,400
The scouts begin the meeting, they conduct their

134
00:08:40,400 --> 00:08:43,440
meeting, they do whatever it is they're going to do,

135
00:08:43,440 --> 00:08:46,560
whatever they've planned, and then they look at you

136
00:08:46,560 --> 00:08:50,080
towards the end and they say, now it's your turn

137
00:08:50,080 --> 00:08:53,120
for a minute.

138
00:08:53,120 --> 00:08:56,000
And you stand up, you give your scout master's minute,

139
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:00,240
say good night scouts, and that's about it.

140
00:09:00,240 --> 00:09:01,760
Now actually, you're not going to be sitting in the

141
00:09:01,760 --> 00:09:03,400
chair the whole time because you're going to have

142
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:05,800
the odd scout master's conference to do.

143
00:09:05,800 --> 00:09:07,800
You're going to be talking with your fellow leaders,

144
00:09:07,800 --> 00:09:11,800
you might be planning your part of an upcoming

145
00:09:11,800 --> 00:09:13,720
outing or something like that.

146
00:09:13,720 --> 00:09:15,440
Just making sure that you have enough tents to

147
00:09:15,440 --> 00:09:19,840
sleep in for the leaders and all that type of thing.

148
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:23,960
But you're not actually engaged and involved in the

149
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,080
ongoing program of that troop meeting.

150
00:09:27,080 --> 00:09:30,320
That might be an unfamiliar picture to some of us.

151
00:09:30,320 --> 00:09:36,120
But I've been on both sides of the dividing line there.

152
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:40,480
I've been actively engaged and involved in running

153
00:09:40,480 --> 00:09:45,240
troop meetings and presenting things for the scouts.

154
00:09:45,240 --> 00:09:49,480
And I've been on the other side where I'm

155
00:09:49,480 --> 00:09:52,240
watching what's going on.

156
00:09:52,240 --> 00:09:54,280
And I'll have a couple of things to talk to my senior

157
00:09:54,280 --> 00:09:56,680
patrol leader about at the end of the meeting.

158
00:09:56,680 --> 00:09:58,360
Did this well?

159
00:09:58,360 --> 00:09:59,680
This didn't go too well.

160
00:09:59,680 --> 00:10:01,360
How are you going to fix that?

161
00:10:01,360 --> 00:10:03,960
What's your plans for next time?

162
00:10:03,960 --> 00:10:07,520
And the boys are so happy when they're actually running

163
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:14,600
the show that they'll come back.

164
00:10:14,600 --> 00:10:16,480
It's not a Nielsen rating kind of thing,

165
00:10:16,480 --> 00:10:18,480
whether they're going, well, the scout master

166
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:20,720
wasn't really on this week.

167
00:10:20,720 --> 00:10:21,920
Yeah, I didn't like that show.

168
00:10:21,959 --> 00:10:23,520
Who wrote that one?

169
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:25,479
That one wasn't very good.

170
00:10:25,479 --> 00:10:27,360
No, they're not an audience.

171
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:28,680
They are the participants.

172
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:32,000
They are the ones that make this happen.

173
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:34,199
And that's what we need to keep in our heads

174
00:10:34,199 --> 00:10:37,000
as we're looking forward into the fall and the winter

175
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:38,959
in our troop meetings.

176
00:10:38,959 --> 00:10:43,240
Well, we're going to wind this up in the next podcast.

177
00:10:43,240 --> 00:10:44,800
We're going to just finish up talking about some

178
00:10:44,800 --> 00:10:49,280
of the broad underlying concepts about what adults do

179
00:10:49,280 --> 00:10:50,240
at a troop meeting.

180
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:45,520
That's right, it's time for a scout master's minute.

181
00:11:45,520 --> 00:11:47,680
One of the founders of the Boy Scouts of America,

182
00:11:47,680 --> 00:11:50,960
Ernest Thompson Seaton, was visiting a camp

183
00:11:50,960 --> 00:11:53,520
he founded near his New Jersey home.

184
00:11:53,520 --> 00:11:56,840
And Seaton had invited several important local businessmen

185
00:11:56,840 --> 00:11:59,320
to join him on this particular visit

186
00:11:59,320 --> 00:12:02,280
to interest them in supporting the camp.

187
00:12:02,280 --> 00:12:04,560
During their stay, they watched with great interest

188
00:12:04,560 --> 00:12:06,560
to some of the scouts there.

189
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:08,160
They probably weren't scouts right then

190
00:12:08,160 --> 00:12:10,160
because I think this was during the period

191
00:12:10,160 --> 00:12:14,400
where Seaton was working on his Woodcraft Indians idea.

192
00:12:14,439 --> 00:12:18,040
So this was probably before the founding of the BSA.

193
00:12:18,040 --> 00:12:21,439
But during their stay, they watched with great interest

194
00:12:21,439 --> 00:12:25,079
as some of the boys tried to light a fire by friction

195
00:12:25,079 --> 00:12:28,120
using the ancient bow and drill method.

196
00:12:28,120 --> 00:12:29,160
Their efforts were great

197
00:12:29,160 --> 00:12:31,760
and the resulting fire was a victory won

198
00:12:31,760 --> 00:12:34,439
over many, many attempts.

199
00:12:34,439 --> 00:12:35,600
As they turned from the scene,

200
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,280
one of the guests turned to Seaton and said,

201
00:12:37,280 --> 00:12:40,960
Mr. Seaton, why in a world of matches

202
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:42,720
do you ask the boys to struggle

203
00:12:42,720 --> 00:12:44,320
with these primitive methods?

204
00:12:45,600 --> 00:12:48,120
Well, Seaton thought about it for a moment

205
00:12:48,120 --> 00:12:50,280
and he pointed at the ground.

206
00:12:50,280 --> 00:12:53,400
You are thinking of the fire that is lit here

207
00:12:53,400 --> 00:12:55,760
and then he placed his hand over his heart

208
00:12:55,760 --> 00:12:59,120
and continued while I am after the fire

209
00:12:59,120 --> 00:13:01,440
that is kindled here in the heart.

210
00:13:02,440 --> 00:13:04,080
The challenges that life present

211
00:13:04,080 --> 00:13:06,120
often turn us from an easy path

212
00:13:06,120 --> 00:13:09,440
to one that requires much more effort and more skill.

213
00:13:10,280 --> 00:13:12,640
As scouts, we deliberately challenge ourselves

214
00:13:12,640 --> 00:13:14,920
to develop our skill as outdoorsmen

215
00:13:14,920 --> 00:13:17,800
and extend our own personal limits.

216
00:13:17,800 --> 00:13:21,080
There's a lot of easier ways to do things,

217
00:13:21,080 --> 00:13:22,760
but the way that offers growth

218
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:24,400
is often the most difficult

219
00:13:25,440 --> 00:13:27,720
and in the end, the most meaningful.

220
00:13:27,720 --> 00:13:30,040
["The Truth"]

221
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:57,440
This has to be the truth, folks,

222
00:13:57,440 --> 00:14:02,440
because there is no way anyone could make this up.

223
00:14:06,680 --> 00:14:11,680
Keith Monroe was the scout master of Troop 2

224
00:14:11,820 --> 00:14:15,280
in Santa Monica, California for the first 42 years

225
00:14:15,280 --> 00:14:17,400
of the troops' existence.

226
00:14:17,400 --> 00:14:21,680
He was also a writer and he wrote for Scouting Magazine,

227
00:14:21,680 --> 00:14:25,120
very involved in scouting until his death in 2003

228
00:14:25,160 --> 00:14:26,280
at the age of 88.

229
00:14:27,720 --> 00:14:32,720
Keith pseudonym for some of his writing was Rice E. Cochran

230
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:37,000
and I have a book called Be Prepared,

231
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,080
The Life and Illusions of a Scout Master.

232
00:14:39,080 --> 00:14:41,800
I've read a couple of excerpts from it

233
00:14:41,800 --> 00:14:44,360
on the podcast before

234
00:14:44,360 --> 00:14:46,320
because some of it is just screamingly funny.

235
00:14:46,320 --> 00:14:48,640
As a matter of fact, I don't know where I got this from

236
00:14:48,640 --> 00:14:52,360
but somebody told me that this book was the basis

237
00:14:52,400 --> 00:14:55,620
of the Clifton Web of movie Mr. Scout Master.

238
00:14:55,620 --> 00:14:59,620
Here's part that I thought you would be interested in.

239
00:15:05,620 --> 00:15:07,440
The littered appearance of our camp grounds

240
00:15:07,440 --> 00:15:09,720
by the end of an outing always exasperated me

241
00:15:09,720 --> 00:15:12,500
and I insisted on thorough cleanups

242
00:15:12,500 --> 00:15:16,680
but at first these took an interminable time.

243
00:15:16,680 --> 00:15:18,760
When our scouts were instructed to walk through camp

244
00:15:18,760 --> 00:15:20,440
and gather up all the trash inside,

245
00:15:20,440 --> 00:15:22,880
they wandered around like the spirits of the dam

246
00:15:22,880 --> 00:15:25,320
aimless and without emotion.

247
00:15:25,320 --> 00:15:27,800
Most of the orange peels and paper stayed on the ground

248
00:15:27,800 --> 00:15:30,760
until I personally planted them out one by one

249
00:15:30,760 --> 00:15:34,080
and directed some scout by name to pick each one of them up.

250
00:15:35,080 --> 00:15:38,280
At last I discovered how to get the job done rapidly.

251
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:40,320
10 minutes before we were ready to depart

252
00:15:40,320 --> 00:15:42,320
I announced the last thing we're gonna do

253
00:15:42,320 --> 00:15:44,680
before we go home is to auction off

254
00:15:44,680 --> 00:15:46,320
these candy bars I have here.

255
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:55,240
The bidding will be by pieces of trash.

256
00:15:55,240 --> 00:15:57,000
Whichever scouts collect the most trash

257
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,160
will be able to bid the highest.

258
00:15:59,160 --> 00:16:02,800
You've got eight minutes to pick up everything you can find.

259
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:05,600
That always worked because 10 minutes later

260
00:16:05,600 --> 00:16:08,200
when the camp as bearers of jackals and scavengers

261
00:16:08,200 --> 00:16:10,200
had been there and when the Victoria scouts

262
00:16:10,200 --> 00:16:12,640
munching their candy bars and when the scout master

263
00:16:12,640 --> 00:16:14,600
smug in the knowledge that he had mastered

264
00:16:14,600 --> 00:16:18,100
his scouts once again, we all went home rejoicing.

265
00:16:21,120 --> 00:16:22,560
You know what I found really funny here

266
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:25,320
because I can picture this in my mind.

267
00:16:33,560 --> 00:16:35,760
When our scouts were instructed to walk through the camp

268
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:37,520
and gather up all the trash inside,

269
00:16:37,520 --> 00:16:40,960
they wandered out about like the spirits of the dam

270
00:16:40,960 --> 00:16:42,840
aimless and without emotion.

271
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:50,080
Anyway, I'm sure we'll find some other interesting things

272
00:16:50,080 --> 00:16:51,120
in this book.

273
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:54,240
Again, it's rice, Cochrane, Be Prepared,

274
00:16:54,240 --> 00:16:56,680
the life and illusions of the scout master

275
00:16:56,680 --> 00:16:59,840
and it is out of print as far as I know.

276
00:16:59,840 --> 00:17:01,560
But it's not that difficult to find

277
00:17:01,560 --> 00:17:03,840
from some of the used book sellers online.

278
00:17:03,840 --> 00:17:10,840
Oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh, oh.

279
00:17:21,120 --> 00:17:23,000
Well, thanks for listening and here's how to keep in touch.

280
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:28,000
You can read the Scout Master blog at scoutmastercg.com.

281
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:31,240
You can also get in touch by email with me, Clark Greene,

282
00:17:31,240 --> 00:17:36,080
at scoutmastercg at Verizon.net.

283
00:17:36,080 --> 00:17:37,080
Now, when you come over to the blog,

284
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:39,280
make sure to subscribe to the Scout Master newsletter,

285
00:17:39,280 --> 00:17:42,120
which is a monthly digest of the Scout Master blog

286
00:17:42,120 --> 00:17:44,400
and podcast with some great scouting resources

287
00:17:44,400 --> 00:17:47,360
around a monthly theme and every newsletter

288
00:17:47,360 --> 00:17:50,920
contains a giveaway from one of our affiliate sponsors.

289
00:17:50,920 --> 00:17:53,720
You can subscribe to the Scout Master podcast on iTunes

290
00:17:53,720 --> 00:17:55,680
and when you do, feel free to leave a comment

291
00:17:55,680 --> 00:17:57,480
or review or a rating.

292
00:17:57,480 --> 00:17:59,880
If you wanna call and hear yourself

293
00:17:59,880 --> 00:18:02,480
on the Scout Master podcast, that's right.

294
00:18:02,480 --> 00:18:05,080
Call in and leave a voicemail comment or question

295
00:18:05,080 --> 00:18:10,080
at 4-8-4-7-3-4-0-0-0-2.

296
00:18:10,080 --> 00:18:12,240
Now, you can follow us at the Scout Master blog

297
00:18:12,240 --> 00:18:16,680
on Facebook and Scout Master CG on Twitter.

298
00:18:16,680 --> 00:18:23,520
Now, the Scout Master blog and the Scout Master podcast

299
00:18:23,520 --> 00:18:26,000
are not official publications of the Boy Scouts of America,

300
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:27,680
nor are they endorsed or sponsored

301
00:18:27,680 --> 00:18:28,880
by the Boy Scouts of America.

302
00:18:28,880 --> 00:18:32,720
Hey, it's just me and I've got a microphone

303
00:18:32,720 --> 00:18:34,280
and I wanna lend a hand to Scout leaders

304
00:18:34,280 --> 00:18:37,280
and maybe have a little bit of fun along the way.

305
00:18:37,280 --> 00:18:40,040
Before we go, we're gonna hear from our founder,

306
00:18:40,040 --> 00:18:41,800
Sir Robert Baden-Powell.

307
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:42,640
Sir Robert.

308
00:18:42,640 --> 00:18:46,920
Good luck to you and good camping.

309
00:18:46,920 --> 00:18:48,560
Why thank you, sir.

310
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:51,360
Now, remember, folks, this is a summer rebroadcast

311
00:18:51,360 --> 00:18:53,800
of some of our favorites from the Scout Master podcast

312
00:18:53,800 --> 00:18:55,000
over the past year.

313
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,240
While we're off camping and traveling,

314
00:18:57,240 --> 00:19:00,040
we won't be able to reply to emails and comments

315
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:02,400
and things like that as quickly as we could

316
00:19:02,400 --> 00:19:03,720
when we're at home.

317
00:19:03,720 --> 00:19:05,760
But give us a couple of weeks

318
00:19:05,760 --> 00:19:08,320
and we'll see you again next time.

